Graded 5 docs in parallel: 0 relevant, 5 irrelevantDocuments: [Document(metadata={'char_count': 694, 'pk': 462470511333799204, 'rerank_score': 0.0006190416612984411}, page_content='For underground installations, questions arise, such as who handles the wiring, conduits, and laying, and who manages excavation and backfilling? These civil tasks must also be clearly defined. The above covers the coordination with the utility company regarding electrical aspects.\n\n说话人1: 02:01:47 - 02:03:43\n\nNow let’s talk about communication, which mainly involves telephone and internet services. In this regard, it’s also under the service tab – whether it’s overhead or MID (Main Intermediate Distribution), and where is this communication room located? How are the incoming lines routed? What size cable is required? Nowadays it’s fiber optic cables, so what diameter conduit is needed?'), Document(metadata={'char_count': 877, 'pk': 462470511333799021, 'rerank_score': 0.0004356301410126391}, page_content='说话人1: 00:11:50 - 00:12:40\n\nWires can be divided into two types: single-core and stranded. Typically, wires of size 8 AWG and above adopt a stranded structure, while wires of size 12 AWG and below use a single-core structure.\n\nConnections between wires must meet specific requirements, which are all basic standards in construction. Additionally, under different environmental conditions, such as waterproof or explosion-proof requirements, corresponding considerations are also necessary.\n\nThe selection criteria for cable and wire sizes will be explained in detail in subsequent slides. This is a basic introduction to wire conduit installation.\n\n说话人1: 00:12:41 - 00:13:27\n\nIn North America, conduit pipes are primarily categorized into several types. One is EMT (Electrical Metallic Tubing), a relatively lightweight piping material; another is Rigid Metallic Tubing Conduit.'), Document(metadata={'char_count': 766, 'pk': 462470511333799590, 'rerank_score': 0.0002148592528014818}, page_content='But mainly, it requires the internal dimensions to be small, so it should definitely fit, then there’s no problem. As long as the other performance indicators can still meet the requirements, then it’s fine.\n\n说话人1: 01:09:46 - 01:10:27\n\nHowever, the materials we submitted might not match this. In that case, which material are you referring to? The wires and cables? Take a distribution box as an example. What we submitted might be a distribution box with a brand label and fixed dimensions—length, width, height (height, width, depth). If it turns out to be too small after later production, do we need to resubmit the scaled drawings and documents for this part? Or do designers generally overlook this and not pay much attention to it?\n\n说话人2: 01:10:27 - 01:10:55'), Document(metadata={'char_count': 967, 'pk': 462470511333799161, 'rerank_score': 0.00011959857805023158}, page_content='The electrical drawings include a drawing index and the legends used. Then this is the site plan, showing the overall situation of the site. The site plan typically needs to accommodate the electrical service entrance, because based on load calculations, the building’s power demand is relatively high. The power utility has regulations—for example, in Vancouver, the maximum allowed is a 1600A 600V service entrance. You can calculate 1600A × 600V × 0.8, which gives around 1200 kW or 1 MW. If the building’s total power demand exceeds this, a high-voltage service entrance is required. So in this case, since it exceeds the limit, a high-voltage entrance is used. Therefore, the building has an indoor substation. The site plan indicates the location of the service entrance—for instance, BC Hydro’s entrance is here. If there’s landscape lighting, it will also be shown on the site plan. Additionally, there’s grounding. See this grounding—we installed a full loop'), Document(metadata={'char_count': 711, 'pk': 462470511333799254, 'rerank_score': 3.6478537675800285e-05}, page_content='Additionally, increasing the conductor cross-sectional area is another solution. However, enlarging the conductor size solely to mitigate voltage drop is not economical. In some cases, there is no alternative, such as when laying a 1,000-meter-long cable to a pump house in an industrial facility.\n\n说话人3: 00:20:47 - 00:24:20\n\nDue to the lack of alternative solutions, wire specifications are often excessively oversized. For instance, when the actual current is only 100 amps, the selected wire may ultimately be rated for 300 amps. This leads to compatibility issues when connecting to end-load terminals—whether it’s circuit breakers or terminal blocks, their sizes struggle to accommodate overly large wires.')]
